{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":1,"id":"code-weight-one-multiset-consolidation-loop","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Code weight: one MultiSet consolidation loop"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reviewed executable revision: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"a27600fd"},{"type":"text","value":" (base "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bef12e24"},{"type":"text","value":", the fetched\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"origin/main"},{"type":"text","value":" at review time). This record follows in a documentation-only\ncommit. The oracle lands in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"35720388"},{"type":"text","value":" on unchanged production code. The\nrefactor lands in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"b1599995"},{"type":"text","value":". Review fixes land in "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"a27600fd"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The work started on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"b5d92ceb"},{"type":"text","value":" and was rebased onto "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bef12e24"},{"type":"text","value":". The\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/db-ivm"},{"type":"text","value":" sources are identical at those two revisions. The base\nmutants and the timings below ran against the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"b5d92ceb"},{"type":"text","value":" sources, and they\napply to "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bef12e24"},{"type":"text","value":" without change."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"change","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Change"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MultiSet.consolidate()"},{"type":"text","value":" had two hand-written loops. The keyed loop handled\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[key, value]"},{"type":"text","value":" multisets. The unkeyed loop handled other data. Both summed\nmultiplicities per identity, kept the first record, and dropped zero sums. One\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"consolidateBy(inner, identityOf)"},{"type":"text","value":" helper now serves both. Only the identity\nfunction differs."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The internal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ObjectIdGenerator"},{"type":"text","value":" class becomes a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getStringId"},{"type":"text","value":" function with\nthe same encoding."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The internal "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"DefaultMap"},{"type":"text","value":" class and its four unit tests are removed. No other\ncode used it. Neither class was exported from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/db-ivm"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A contract comment on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"consolidate()"},{"type":"text","value":" replaces the removed method comments."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The change is intended to preserve behavior, including the keyed identity\ncollisions in "},{"type":"link","href":"https://github.com/TanStack/db/issues/1948","children":[{"type":"text","value":"#1948"}]},{"type":"text","value":". A fix for\nthat bug is a separate behavior change."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"b1599995"},{"type":"text","value":" changed one behavior. On the single-primitive path, the base code\nreturned the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Map"},{"type":"text","value":" key, so a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"-0"},{"type":"text","value":" record came back as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"b1599995"},{"type":"text","value":" returned\nthe input record, so "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"-0"},{"type":"text","value":" stayed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"-0"},{"type":"text","value":". The oracle did not generate "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"-0"},{"type":"text","value":", and\nno other db-ivm test failed. Review found the change. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"a27600fd"},{"type":"text","value":" returns the\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Map"},{"type":"text","value":" key again, and the oracle grammar now contains "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"-0"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NaN"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,"right","right","right"],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Entry"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Revision"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"min"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"gzip"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"brotli"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"full "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/db"},{"type":"text","value":" (db-ivm inlined)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bef12e24"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"375,166"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"105,868"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"89,634"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"a27600fd"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"374,186"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"105,541"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"89,418"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Δ"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"−980"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"−327"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"−216"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"standalone "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"@tanstack/db-ivm"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"bef12e24"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"35,345"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"10,894"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"9,855"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"a27600fd"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"34,374"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"10,570"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"9,513"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Δ"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"−971"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"−324"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"−342"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"All numbers come from an esbuild bundle of the public entry, minified."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"why-a-new-owner","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Why a new owner"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Before this change, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MultiSet.consolidate"},{"type":"text","value":" had no owner in the coverage map.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tests/multiset.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":" checks primitive and string values only. The existing\ndb-ivm suite rejects each wrong design below, but no test states the identity\nrules that the refactor must keep. The new oracle,\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/db-ivm/tests/multiset-consolidate-oracle.property.test.ts"},{"type":"text","value":", states\nthem and checks them against an independent model."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"contract-path-and-limits","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Contract, path, and limits"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Authority: the contract comment on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MultiSet.consolidate()"},{"type":"text","value":". It restates the\nbehavior and the keyed and unkeyed method comments of "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"b5d92ceb"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keyed."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Every record is a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[key, value]"},{"type":"text","value":" pair with a string or number\nkey. The key compares by value. A primitive value compares by value. An\nobject value compares by reference. A value that is an array of length 2 is\na join tuple, and its two elements compare by the same rule."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Unkeyed, one primitive type."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Every record is a string, or every record is\na number. Records compare as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Map"},{"type":"text","value":" keys do: "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"-0"},{"type":"text","value":" equals "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NaN"},{"type":"text","value":"\nequals "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NaN"},{"type":"text","value":". The result holds "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0"},{"type":"text","value":" for a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"-0"},{"type":"text","value":" record."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Unkeyed, structural."}]},{"type":"text","value":" Any other multiset. Records compare by structure."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For each identity, the result holds one record: the first in input order\n(normalized as in rule 2), with the summed multiplicity. An identity whose sum is zero is absent. The input is\nnot changed."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The model computes identity with a type-tagged key and a local reference map.\nIt does not import "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"getStringId"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"ObjectIdGenerator"},{"type":"text","value":", or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hash"},{"type":"text","value":". The driver\ncalls "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"new MultiSet(records).consolidate().getInner()"},{"type":"text","value":". The refinement check\nrecords every output entry, so duplicates, extra identities, and missing\nidentities stay visible. It compares each multiplicity with the modeled sum and\neach retained record with the modeled first record by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Object.is"},{"type":"text","value":". It then\nchecks that every input pair still holds its original record and multiplicity,\nand that a tagged encoding of all record contents did not change."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Limits:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The oracle does not assert output order. No contract states it."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keyed identity is a text encoding that is not injective (#1948). The grammar\nexcludes each known collision: keys or values of different types with the\nsame text ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'1'"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'true'"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1n"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1"},{"type":"text","value":"), keys or values\nthat contain "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"|"},{"type":"text","value":", and symbol or function values, which compare by text. The\nexclusions must go when that bug is fixed."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Structural identity uses a 32-bit hash in production. The small domain makes\na collision unlikely, but the oracle does not claim injectivity."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Generated objects have one key, so key-order sensitivity is outside this law."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"grammar-controls-and-calibration","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Grammar controls and calibration"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The grammar has five modes:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"keyed"},{"type":"text","value":": keys from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0, 1, 2, 'a', 'b'"},{"type":"text","value":". Values are primitives (including\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"-0"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NaN"},{"type":"text","value":"), three shared\nobjects (two with equal contents), fresh join tuples of those leaves, or fresh\narrays of length 3."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"numbers"},{"type":"text","value":": "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0, -0, 1, 2, 3, NaN"},{"type":"text","value":". "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"strings"},{"type":"text","value":": "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'p', 'q', 'r'"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"structural"},{"type":"text","value":": "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'1'"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"true"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"null"},{"type":"text","value":", fresh one-key objects, and fresh\narrays of length 2."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fallback"},{"type":"text","value":": keyed records plus one appended non-keyed object, which makes the\nwhole multiset unkeyed."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Multiplicities are integers from −2 to 2, so zero multiplicities and cancelling\nsums occur."}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reconstruction:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" six pinned witnesses reconstruct each identity rule:\nreference identity, tuple unpacking, other array lengths, the fallback,\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"-0"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"NaN"},{"type":"text","value":" on the single-number path, and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"1"},{"type":"text","value":" versus "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"'1'"},{"type":"text","value":" on the\nstructural path."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Ablation:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" each mode has a distinct rule. The mutants below show that\nremoving a rule's distinction fails the oracle."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Range:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" empty multisets occur in the keyed, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"numbers"},{"type":"text","value":", and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"strings"},{"type":"text","value":"\nmodes. The fixed campaign reaches every mode. In that campaign, more than 30\nof 300 histories merge or cancel records."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Exclusion:"}]},{"type":"text","value":" the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fallback"},{"type":"text","value":" mode checks that one non-keyed record makes the\nkeyed rule inapplicable."}]}]}]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"source-mutants-on-unchanged-production-b5d92ceb","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Source mutants on unchanged production ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"b5d92ceb"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"\"Suite\" means the rest of the db-ivm suite. The column shows failed tests over\ntotal tests."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mutant"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"New oracle"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Suite"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"W1: keyed values compare by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hash"},{"type":"text","value":" (contents)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (4/7)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (4/591)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"W2: join tuples compare by reference"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (2/7)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (47/591)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"W3: keyed loop skips a non-keyed record instead of falling back"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (3/7)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (3/591)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"W4a: keyed result keeps zero sums"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (3/7)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (18/591)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"W4b: unkeyed result keeps zero sums"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (2/7)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (5/591)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"W5: the last record replaces the first"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (3/7)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (3/591)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"W6: mixed unkeyed data uses raw values as keys"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (3/7)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (4/591)"}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"source-mutants-on-the-refactor-b1599995","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Source mutants on the refactor ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"b1599995"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mutant"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"New oracle"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Suite"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"N1: keyed values compare by "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hash"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (4/8)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (4/588)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"N2: join tuples are not unpacked"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (2/8)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (47/588)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"N3: keyed detection checks only the first record"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"failure by crash (3/8)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"failure by crash (3/588)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"N4: zero sums are kept"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (3/8)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (21/588)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"N5: the last record replaces the first"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (4/8)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (4/588)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"N6: mixed unkeyed data uses raw values as keys"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (3/8)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (4/588)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"W7: an input pair becomes the output entry"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (6/8)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (14/588)"}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":3,"id":"source-mutants-on-the-reviewed-head-a27600fd","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Source mutants on the reviewed head ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"a27600fd"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mutant"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"New oracle"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Suite"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"N7: the single-primitive path returns the input record ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"b1599995"},{"type":"text","value":")"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (3/9)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (3/589, oracle only)"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"N8: the keyed path writes a "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"-0"},{"type":"text","value":" value as "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"0"},{"type":"text","value":" in the input pair"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (2/9)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"assertion failure (2/589, oracle only)"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The N1–N6 and W7 counts come from "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"b1599995"},{"type":"text","value":", which had 8 oracle cases.\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"a27600fd"},{"type":"text","value":" changes only the single-primitive result and the loop that builds\nthe result, so those mutants apply unchanged. N7 and N8 survive the rest of\nthe db-ivm suite. N8 changes no output record, so only the input contents check\nrejects it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"N3 fails because the keyed path destructures a non-pair record and throws a\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TypeError"},{"type":"text","value":". This rejects the design, but by crash, not by a value assertion.\nW7 is a risk that only the refactor introduces. Summing into a reused input pair\nchanges the input, and the input-immutability check rejects it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each oracle run executes two campaigns of 300 generated histories each (fixed\nseed and random), the positive execution witness, and six pinned witnesses."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"performance","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Performance"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Consolidation runs on every graph step, so the change was timed. Each process\nloads one implementation and times one workload set. Processes alternate\nbetween implementations, and the comparison uses the median over five\nprocesses of each."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A first same-process harness showed a position bias. An identical copy of the\nbase code measured 6% to 22% slower when it loaded second. The\nprocess-per-implementation harness removed that bias. Its A/A control measured\n±2% on most workloads and up to ±7% on hash-heavy unkeyed objects."}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,"right","right","right"],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Workload"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"n=10"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"n=1,000"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"n=100,000"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"keyed rows (update pairs)"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0.683"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0.830"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0.865"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"keyed join tuples"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0.773"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0.810"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0.894"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"keyed primitive values"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0.634"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0.743"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0.836"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"unkeyed numbers"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0.991"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"1.008"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0.763"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"unkeyed objects"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0.821"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0.802"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"0.824"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each ratio is "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"a27600fd"},{"type":"text","value":" time over base time. Lower is faster. Unkeyed numbers\nat sizes 10 and 1,000 are inside the A/A noise band. Batches of size 10\nran 20,000 times, size 1,000 ran 200 times, and size 100,000 ran twice. Output\nsizes matched in every workload. The refactor keeps one "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Map"},{"type":"text","value":" of\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[record, sum]"},{"type":"text","value":" entries per identity. The base code kept two parallel maps. The\nharness is not checked in."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"orc-001-through-orc-012","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001 through ORC-012"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Requirement"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Result"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-001"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The oracle's opening comment states the three identity rules, the retained record, zero-sum removal, input immutability, the authority, and the limits above."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-002"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The model uses its own type-tagged identity and reference map. It does not import production identity helpers or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"hash"},{"type":"text","value":". It restates the keyed/unkeyed classification because the contract comment on "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"consolidate()"},{"type":"text","value":" states that classification. The "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fallback"},{"type":"text","value":" mode and mutants W3 and N3 attack that boundary."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-003"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The contract, model ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"expectedConsolidation"},{"type":"text","value":"), grammar ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"historyArb"},{"type":"text","value":", "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"buildRecords"},{"type":"text","value":"), driver, and refinement check ("},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"expectConsolidation"},{"type":"text","value":") are separate and visible in the oracle file."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-004"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reconstruction, ablation, range, and exclusion appear above."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-005"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The driver calls the public "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"MultiSet"},{"type":"text","value":" API. The positive witness shows that the fixed campaign reaches all five modes and many merges."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-006"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The source mutants above have their outcome classes."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-007"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One property runs a fixed campaign (seed "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"2026929"},{"type":"text","value":") and a random campaign with the same grammar, check, and budget (300 runs). "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TANSTACK_DB_IVM_CONSOLIDATE_SEED"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"TANSTACK_DB_IVM_CONSOLIDATE_PATH"},{"type":"text","value":" select a direct replay. The property does not use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fc.commands"},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-008"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The model is a stateless recomputation, so this requirement does not apply."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-009"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Model terms (keyed, unkeyed, identity, retained record) name production concepts directly."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-010"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"fast-check reports the seed, path, and shrunk history. Assertion messages name the identity that diverged. No external resources are acquired."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-011"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"No shared semantic fault calls for a second formulation. The #1948 collisions are known faults, and the grammar excludes them explicitly instead of sharing them silently."}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ORC-012"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"This record ties the reviewed revisions, outcome classes, limits, and performance evidence to the coverage map."}]}]]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"verification","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Verification"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"On "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"a27600fd"},{"type":"text","value":", the following passed:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/db-ivm"},{"type":"text","value":" Vitest: 41 files, 589 tests, no type errors."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/db-ivm"},{"type":"text","value":" "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"tsc --noEmit"},{"type":"text","value":": no errors."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"packages/db"},{"type":"text","value":" Vitest, typecheck off: 193 files, 6,830 tests."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pnpm test:oracles"},{"type":"text","value":": 49 files, 2,838 tests."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"pnpm test:minified-db"},{"type":"text","value":": error names, index metadata, query rows, and live\nupdates."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The environment was Node "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"v24.19.0"},{"type":"text","value":" and Vitest "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"3.2.4"},{"type":"text","value":" on Darwin arm64."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"code-weight-one-multiset-consolidation-loop","text":"Code weight: one MultiSet consolidation loop","level":1},{"id":"change","text":"Change","level":2},{"id":"why-a-new-owner","text":"Why a new owner","level":2},{"id":"contract-path-and-limits","text":"Contract, path, and limits","level":2},{"id":"grammar-controls-and-calibration","text":"Grammar controls and calibration","level":2},{"id":"source-mutants-on-unchanged-production-b5d92ceb","text":"Source mutants on unchanged production (b5d92ceb)","level":3},{"id":"source-mutants-on-the-refactor-b1599995","text":"Source mutants on the refactor (b1599995)","level":3},{"id":"source-mutants-on-the-reviewed-head-a27600fd","text":"Source mutants on the reviewed head (a27600fd)","level":3},{"id":"performance","text":"Performance","level":2},{"id":"orc-001-through-orc-012","text":"ORC-001 through ORC-012","level":2},{"id":"verification","text":"Verification","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"# Code weight: one MultiSet consolidation loop\n\nReviewed executable revision: `a27600fd` (base `bef12e24`, the fetched\n`origin/main` at review time). This record follows in a documentation-only\ncommit. The oracle lands in `35720388` on unchanged production code. The\nrefactor lands in `b1599995`. Review fixes land in `a27600fd`.\n\nThe work started on `b5d92ceb` and was rebased onto `bef12e24`. The\n`packages/db-ivm` sources are identical at those two revisions. The base\nmutants and the timings below ran against the `b5d92ceb` sources, and they\napply to `bef12e24` without change.\n\n## Change\n\n`MultiSet.consolidate()` had two hand-written loops. The keyed loop handled\n`[key, value]` multisets. The unkeyed loop handled other data. Both summed\nmultiplicities per identity, kept the first record, and dropped zero sums. One\n`consolidateBy(inner, identityOf)` helper now serves both. Only the identity\nfunction differs.\n\n- The internal `ObjectIdGenerator` class becomes a `getStringId` function with\n  the same encoding.\n- The internal `DefaultMap` class and its four unit tests are removed. No other\n  code used it. Neither class was exported from `@tanstack/db-ivm`.\n- A contract comment on `consolidate()` replaces the removed method comments.\n\nThe change is intended to preserve behavior, including the keyed identity\ncollisions in [#1948](https://github.com/TanStack/db/issues/1948). A fix for\nthat bug is a separate behavior change.\n\n`b1599995` changed one behavior. On the single-primitive path, the base code\nreturned the `Map` key, so a `-0` record came back as `0`. `b1599995` returned\nthe input record, so `-0` stayed `-0`. The oracle did not generate `-0`, and\nno other db-ivm test failed. Review found the change. `a27600fd` returns the\n`Map` key again, and the oracle grammar now contains `-0` and `NaN`.\n\n| Entry | Revision | min | gzip | brotli |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| full `@tanstack/db` (db-ivm inlined) | `bef12e24` | 375,166 | 105,868 | 89,634 |\n| | `a27600fd` | 374,186 | 105,541 | 89,418 |\n| | Δ | −980 | −327 | −216 |\n| standalone `@tanstack/db-ivm` | `bef12e24` | 35,345 | 10,894 | 9,855 |\n| | `a27600fd` | 34,374 | 10,570 | 9,513 |\n| | Δ | −971 | −324 | −342 |\n\nAll numbers come from an esbuild bundle of the public entry, minified.\n\n## Why a new owner\n\nBefore this change, `MultiSet.consolidate` had no owner in the coverage map.\n`tests/multiset.test.ts` checks primitive and string values only. The existing\ndb-ivm suite rejects each wrong design below, but no test states the identity\nrules that the refactor must keep. The new oracle,\n`packages/db-ivm/tests/multiset-consolidate-oracle.property.test.ts`, states\nthem and checks them against an independent model.\n\n## Contract, path, and limits\n\nAuthority: the contract comment on `MultiSet.consolidate()`. It restates the\nbehavior and the keyed and unkeyed method comments of `b5d92ceb`.\n\n1. **Keyed.** Every record is a `[key, value]` pair with a string or number\n   key. The key compares by value. A primitive value compares by value. An\n   object value compares by reference. A value that is an array of length 2 is\n   a join tuple, and its two elements compare by the same rule.\n2. **Unkeyed, one primitive type.** Every record is a string, or every record is\n   a number. Records compare as `Map` keys do: `-0` equals `0`, and `NaN`\n   equals `NaN`. The result holds `0` for a `-0` record.\n3. **Unkeyed, structural.** Any other multiset. Records compare by structure.\n\nFor each identity, the result holds one record: the first in input order\n(normalized as in rule 2), with the summed multiplicity. An identity whose sum is zero is absent. The input is\nnot changed.\n\nThe model computes identity with a type-tagged key and a local reference map.\nIt does not import `getStringId`, `ObjectIdGenerator`, or `hash`. The driver\ncalls `new MultiSet(records).consolidate().getInner()`. The refinement check\nrecords every output entry, so duplicates, extra identities, and missing\nidentities stay visible. It compares each multiplicity with the modeled sum and\neach retained record with the modeled first record by `Object.is`. It then\nchecks that every input pair still holds its original record and multiplicity,\nand that a tagged encoding of all record contents did not change.\n\nLimits:\n\n- The oracle does not assert output order. No contract states it.\n- Keyed identity is a text encoding that is not injective (#1948). The grammar\n  excludes each known collision: keys or values of different types with the\n  same text (`1` and `'1'`, `true` and `'true'`, `1n` and `1`), keys or values\n  that contain `|`, and symbol or function values, which compare by text. The\n  exclusions must go when that bug is fixed.\n- Structural identity uses a 32-bit hash in production. The small domain makes\n  a collision unlikely, but the oracle does not claim injectivity.\n- Generated objects have one key, so key-order sensitivity is outside this law.\n\n## Grammar controls and calibration\n\nThe grammar has five modes:\n\n- `keyed`: keys from `0, 1, 2, 'a', 'b'`. Values are primitives (including\n  `-0` and `NaN`), three shared\n  objects (two with equal contents), fresh join tuples of those leaves, or fresh\n  arrays of length 3.\n- `numbers`: `0, -0, 1, 2, 3, NaN`. `strings`: `'p', 'q', 'r'`.\n- `structural`: `1`, `'1'`, `true`, `null`, fresh one-key objects, and fresh\n  arrays of length 2.\n- `fallback`: keyed records plus one appended non-keyed object, which makes the\n  whole multiset unkeyed.\n\nMultiplicities are integers from −2 to 2, so zero multiplicities and cancelling\nsums occur.\n\n- **Reconstruction:** six pinned witnesses reconstruct each identity rule:\n  reference identity, tuple unpacking, other array lengths, the fallback,\n  `-0` and `NaN` on the single-number path, and `1` versus `'1'` on the\n  structural path.\n- **Ablation:** each mode has a distinct rule. The mutants below show that\n  removing a rule's distinction fails the oracle.\n- **Range:** empty multisets occur in the keyed, `numbers`, and `strings`\n  modes. The fixed campaign reaches every mode. In that campaign, more than 30\n  of 300 histories merge or cancel records.\n- **Exclusion:** the `fallback` mode checks that one non-keyed record makes the\n  keyed rule inapplicable.\n\n### Source mutants on unchanged production (`b5d92ceb`)\n\n\"Suite\" means the rest of the db-ivm suite. The column shows failed tests over\ntotal tests.\n\n| Mutant | New oracle | Suite |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| W1: keyed values compare by `hash` (contents) | assertion failure (4/7) | assertion failure (4/591) |\n| W2: join tuples compare by reference | assertion failure (2/7) | assertion failure (47/591) |\n| W3: keyed loop skips a non-keyed record instead of falling back | assertion failure (3/7) | assertion failure (3/591) |\n| W4a: keyed result keeps zero sums | assertion failure (3/7) | assertion failure (18/591) |\n| W4b: unkeyed result keeps zero sums | assertion failure (2/7) | assertion failure (5/591) |\n| W5: the last record replaces the first | assertion failure (3/7) | assertion failure (3/591) |\n| W6: mixed unkeyed data uses raw values as keys | assertion failure (3/7) | assertion failure (4/591) |\n\n### Source mutants on the refactor (`b1599995`)\n\n| Mutant | New oracle | Suite |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| N1: keyed values compare by `hash` | assertion failure (4/8) | assertion failure (4/588) |\n| N2: join tuples are not unpacked | assertion failure (2/8) | assertion failure (47/588) |\n| N3: keyed detection checks only the first record | failure by crash (3/8) | failure by crash (3/588) |\n| N4: zero sums are kept | assertion failure (3/8) | assertion failure (21/588) |\n| N5: the last record replaces the first | assertion failure (4/8) | assertion failure (4/588) |\n| N6: mixed unkeyed data uses raw values as keys | assertion failure (3/8) | assertion failure (4/588) |\n| W7: an input pair becomes the output entry | assertion failure (6/8) | assertion failure (14/588) |\n\n### Source mutants on the reviewed head (`a27600fd`)\n\n| Mutant | New oracle | Suite |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| N7: the single-primitive path returns the input record (`b1599995`) | assertion failure (3/9) | assertion failure (3/589, oracle only) |\n| N8: the keyed path writes a `-0` value as `0` in the input pair | assertion failure (2/9) | assertion failure (2/589, oracle only) |\n\nThe N1–N6 and W7 counts come from `b1599995`, which had 8 oracle cases.\n`a27600fd` changes only the single-primitive result and the loop that builds\nthe result, so those mutants apply unchanged. N7 and N8 survive the rest of\nthe db-ivm suite. N8 changes no output record, so only the input contents check\nrejects it.\n\nN3 fails because the keyed path destructures a non-pair record and throws a\n`TypeError`. This rejects the design, but by crash, not by a value assertion.\nW7 is a risk that only the refactor introduces. Summing into a reused input pair\nchanges the input, and the input-immutability check rejects it.\n\nEach oracle run executes two campaigns of 300 generated histories each (fixed\nseed and random), the positive execution witness, and six pinned witnesses.\n\n## Performance\n\nConsolidation runs on every graph step, so the change was timed. Each process\nloads one implementation and times one workload set. Processes alternate\nbetween implementations, and the comparison uses the median over five\nprocesses of each.\n\nA first same-process harness showed a position bias. An identical copy of the\nbase code measured 6% to 22% slower when it loaded second. The\nprocess-per-implementation harness removed that bias. Its A/A control measured\n±2% on most workloads and up to ±7% on hash-heavy unkeyed objects.\n\n| Workload | n=10 | n=1,000 | n=100,000 |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| keyed rows (update pairs) | 0.683 | 0.830 | 0.865 |\n| keyed join tuples | 0.773 | 0.810 | 0.894 |\n| keyed primitive values | 0.634 | 0.743 | 0.836 |\n| unkeyed numbers | 0.991 | 1.008 | 0.763 |\n| unkeyed objects | 0.821 | 0.802 | 0.824 |\n\nEach ratio is `a27600fd` time over base time. Lower is faster. Unkeyed numbers\nat sizes 10 and 1,000 are inside the A/A noise band. Batches of size 10\nran 20,000 times, size 1,000 ran 200 times, and size 100,000 ran twice. Output\nsizes matched in every workload. The refactor keeps one `Map` of\n`[record, sum]` entries per identity. The base code kept two parallel maps. The\nharness is not checked in.\n\n## ORC-001 through ORC-012\n\n| Requirement | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 | The oracle's opening comment states the three identity rules, the retained record, zero-sum removal, input immutability, the authority, and the limits above. |\n| ORC-002 | The model uses its own type-tagged identity and reference map. It does not import production identity helpers or `hash`. It restates the keyed/unkeyed classification because the contract comment on `consolidate()` states that classification. The `fallback` mode and mutants W3 and N3 attack that boundary. |\n| ORC-003 | The contract, model (`expectedConsolidation`), grammar (`historyArb`, `buildRecords`), driver, and refinement check (`expectConsolidation`) are separate and visible in the oracle file. |\n| ORC-004 | Reconstruction, ablation, range, and exclusion appear above. |\n| ORC-005 | The driver calls the public `MultiSet` API. The positive witness shows that the fixed campaign reaches all five modes and many merges. |\n| ORC-006 | The source mutants above have their outcome classes. |\n| ORC-007 | One property runs a fixed campaign (seed `2026929`) and a random campaign with the same grammar, check, and budget (300 runs). `TANSTACK_DB_IVM_CONSOLIDATE_SEED` and `TANSTACK_DB_IVM_CONSOLIDATE_PATH` select a direct replay. The property does not use `fc.commands`. |\n| ORC-008 | The model is a stateless recomputation, so this requirement does not apply. |\n| ORC-009 | Model terms (keyed, unkeyed, identity, retained record) name production concepts directly. |\n| ORC-010 | fast-check reports the seed, path, and shrunk history. Assertion messages name the identity that diverged. No external resources are acquired. |\n| ORC-011 | No shared semantic fault calls for a second formulation. The #1948 collisions are known faults, and the grammar excludes them explicitly instead of sharing them silently. |\n| ORC-012 | This record ties the reviewed revisions, outcome classes, limits, and performance evidence to the coverage map. |\n\n## Verification\n\nOn `a27600fd`, the following passed:\n\n- `packages/db-ivm` Vitest: 41 files, 589 tests, no type errors.\n- `packages/db-ivm` `tsc --noEmit`: no errors.\n- `packages/db` Vitest, typecheck off: 193 files, 6,830 tests.\n- `pnpm test:oracles`: 49 files, 2,838 tests.\n- `pnpm test:minified-db`: error names, index metadata, query rows, and live\n  updates.\n\nThe environment was Node `v24.19.0` and Vitest `3.2.4` on Darwin arm64.\n","originalRawMarkdown":"# Code weight: one MultiSet consolidation loop\n\nReviewed executable revision: `a27600fd` (base `bef12e24`, the fetched\n`origin/main` at review time). This record follows in a documentation-only\ncommit. The oracle lands in `35720388` on unchanged production code. The\nrefactor lands in `b1599995`. Review fixes land in `a27600fd`.\n\nThe work started on `b5d92ceb` and was rebased onto `bef12e24`. The\n`packages/db-ivm` sources are identical at those two revisions. The base\nmutants and the timings below ran against the `b5d92ceb` sources, and they\napply to `bef12e24` without change.\n\n## Change\n\n`MultiSet.consolidate()` had two hand-written loops. The keyed loop handled\n`[key, value]` multisets. The unkeyed loop handled other data. Both summed\nmultiplicities per identity, kept the first record, and dropped zero sums. One\n`consolidateBy(inner, identityOf)` helper now serves both. Only the identity\nfunction differs.\n\n- The internal `ObjectIdGenerator` class becomes a `getStringId` function with\n  the same encoding.\n- The internal `DefaultMap` class and its four unit tests are removed. No other\n  code used it. Neither class was exported from `@tanstack/db-ivm`.\n- A contract comment on `consolidate()` replaces the removed method comments.\n\nThe change is intended to preserve behavior, including the keyed identity\ncollisions in [#1948](https://github.com/TanStack/db/issues/1948). A fix for\nthat bug is a separate behavior change.\n\n`b1599995` changed one behavior. On the single-primitive path, the base code\nreturned the `Map` key, so a `-0` record came back as `0`. `b1599995` returned\nthe input record, so `-0` stayed `-0`. The oracle did not generate `-0`, and\nno other db-ivm test failed. Review found the change. `a27600fd` returns the\n`Map` key again, and the oracle grammar now contains `-0` and `NaN`.\n\n| Entry | Revision | min | gzip | brotli |\n| --- | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| full `@tanstack/db` (db-ivm inlined) | `bef12e24` | 375,166 | 105,868 | 89,634 |\n| | `a27600fd` | 374,186 | 105,541 | 89,418 |\n| | Δ | −980 | −327 | −216 |\n| standalone `@tanstack/db-ivm` | `bef12e24` | 35,345 | 10,894 | 9,855 |\n| | `a27600fd` | 34,374 | 10,570 | 9,513 |\n| | Δ | −971 | −324 | −342 |\n\nAll numbers come from an esbuild bundle of the public entry, minified.\n\n## Why a new owner\n\nBefore this change, `MultiSet.consolidate` had no owner in the coverage map.\n`tests/multiset.test.ts` checks primitive and string values only. The existing\ndb-ivm suite rejects each wrong design below, but no test states the identity\nrules that the refactor must keep. The new oracle,\n`packages/db-ivm/tests/multiset-consolidate-oracle.property.test.ts`, states\nthem and checks them against an independent model.\n\n## Contract, path, and limits\n\nAuthority: the contract comment on `MultiSet.consolidate()`. It restates the\nbehavior and the keyed and unkeyed method comments of `b5d92ceb`.\n\n1. **Keyed.** Every record is a `[key, value]` pair with a string or number\n   key. The key compares by value. A primitive value compares by value. An\n   object value compares by reference. A value that is an array of length 2 is\n   a join tuple, and its two elements compare by the same rule.\n2. **Unkeyed, one primitive type.** Every record is a string, or every record is\n   a number. Records compare as `Map` keys do: `-0` equals `0`, and `NaN`\n   equals `NaN`. The result holds `0` for a `-0` record.\n3. **Unkeyed, structural.** Any other multiset. Records compare by structure.\n\nFor each identity, the result holds one record: the first in input order\n(normalized as in rule 2), with the summed multiplicity. An identity whose sum is zero is absent. The input is\nnot changed.\n\nThe model computes identity with a type-tagged key and a local reference map.\nIt does not import `getStringId`, `ObjectIdGenerator`, or `hash`. The driver\ncalls `new MultiSet(records).consolidate().getInner()`. The refinement check\nrecords every output entry, so duplicates, extra identities, and missing\nidentities stay visible. It compares each multiplicity with the modeled sum and\neach retained record with the modeled first record by `Object.is`. It then\nchecks that every input pair still holds its original record and multiplicity,\nand that a tagged encoding of all record contents did not change.\n\nLimits:\n\n- The oracle does not assert output order. No contract states it.\n- Keyed identity is a text encoding that is not injective (#1948). The grammar\n  excludes each known collision: keys or values of different types with the\n  same text (`1` and `'1'`, `true` and `'true'`, `1n` and `1`), keys or values\n  that contain `|`, and symbol or function values, which compare by text. The\n  exclusions must go when that bug is fixed.\n- Structural identity uses a 32-bit hash in production. The small domain makes\n  a collision unlikely, but the oracle does not claim injectivity.\n- Generated objects have one key, so key-order sensitivity is outside this law.\n\n## Grammar controls and calibration\n\nThe grammar has five modes:\n\n- `keyed`: keys from `0, 1, 2, 'a', 'b'`. Values are primitives (including\n  `-0` and `NaN`), three shared\n  objects (two with equal contents), fresh join tuples of those leaves, or fresh\n  arrays of length 3.\n- `numbers`: `0, -0, 1, 2, 3, NaN`. `strings`: `'p', 'q', 'r'`.\n- `structural`: `1`, `'1'`, `true`, `null`, fresh one-key objects, and fresh\n  arrays of length 2.\n- `fallback`: keyed records plus one appended non-keyed object, which makes the\n  whole multiset unkeyed.\n\nMultiplicities are integers from −2 to 2, so zero multiplicities and cancelling\nsums occur.\n\n- **Reconstruction:** six pinned witnesses reconstruct each identity rule:\n  reference identity, tuple unpacking, other array lengths, the fallback,\n  `-0` and `NaN` on the single-number path, and `1` versus `'1'` on the\n  structural path.\n- **Ablation:** each mode has a distinct rule. The mutants below show that\n  removing a rule's distinction fails the oracle.\n- **Range:** empty multisets occur in the keyed, `numbers`, and `strings`\n  modes. The fixed campaign reaches every mode. In that campaign, more than 30\n  of 300 histories merge or cancel records.\n- **Exclusion:** the `fallback` mode checks that one non-keyed record makes the\n  keyed rule inapplicable.\n\n### Source mutants on unchanged production (`b5d92ceb`)\n\n\"Suite\" means the rest of the db-ivm suite. The column shows failed tests over\ntotal tests.\n\n| Mutant | New oracle | Suite |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| W1: keyed values compare by `hash` (contents) | assertion failure (4/7) | assertion failure (4/591) |\n| W2: join tuples compare by reference | assertion failure (2/7) | assertion failure (47/591) |\n| W3: keyed loop skips a non-keyed record instead of falling back | assertion failure (3/7) | assertion failure (3/591) |\n| W4a: keyed result keeps zero sums | assertion failure (3/7) | assertion failure (18/591) |\n| W4b: unkeyed result keeps zero sums | assertion failure (2/7) | assertion failure (5/591) |\n| W5: the last record replaces the first | assertion failure (3/7) | assertion failure (3/591) |\n| W6: mixed unkeyed data uses raw values as keys | assertion failure (3/7) | assertion failure (4/591) |\n\n### Source mutants on the refactor (`b1599995`)\n\n| Mutant | New oracle | Suite |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| N1: keyed values compare by `hash` | assertion failure (4/8) | assertion failure (4/588) |\n| N2: join tuples are not unpacked | assertion failure (2/8) | assertion failure (47/588) |\n| N3: keyed detection checks only the first record | failure by crash (3/8) | failure by crash (3/588) |\n| N4: zero sums are kept | assertion failure (3/8) | assertion failure (21/588) |\n| N5: the last record replaces the first | assertion failure (4/8) | assertion failure (4/588) |\n| N6: mixed unkeyed data uses raw values as keys | assertion failure (3/8) | assertion failure (4/588) |\n| W7: an input pair becomes the output entry | assertion failure (6/8) | assertion failure (14/588) |\n\n### Source mutants on the reviewed head (`a27600fd`)\n\n| Mutant | New oracle | Suite |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| N7: the single-primitive path returns the input record (`b1599995`) | assertion failure (3/9) | assertion failure (3/589, oracle only) |\n| N8: the keyed path writes a `-0` value as `0` in the input pair | assertion failure (2/9) | assertion failure (2/589, oracle only) |\n\nThe N1–N6 and W7 counts come from `b1599995`, which had 8 oracle cases.\n`a27600fd` changes only the single-primitive result and the loop that builds\nthe result, so those mutants apply unchanged. N7 and N8 survive the rest of\nthe db-ivm suite. N8 changes no output record, so only the input contents check\nrejects it.\n\nN3 fails because the keyed path destructures a non-pair record and throws a\n`TypeError`. This rejects the design, but by crash, not by a value assertion.\nW7 is a risk that only the refactor introduces. Summing into a reused input pair\nchanges the input, and the input-immutability check rejects it.\n\nEach oracle run executes two campaigns of 300 generated histories each (fixed\nseed and random), the positive execution witness, and six pinned witnesses.\n\n## Performance\n\nConsolidation runs on every graph step, so the change was timed. Each process\nloads one implementation and times one workload set. Processes alternate\nbetween implementations, and the comparison uses the median over five\nprocesses of each.\n\nA first same-process harness showed a position bias. An identical copy of the\nbase code measured 6% to 22% slower when it loaded second. The\nprocess-per-implementation harness removed that bias. Its A/A control measured\n±2% on most workloads and up to ±7% on hash-heavy unkeyed objects.\n\n| Workload | n=10 | n=1,000 | n=100,000 |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| keyed rows (update pairs) | 0.683 | 0.830 | 0.865 |\n| keyed join tuples | 0.773 | 0.810 | 0.894 |\n| keyed primitive values | 0.634 | 0.743 | 0.836 |\n| unkeyed numbers | 0.991 | 1.008 | 0.763 |\n| unkeyed objects | 0.821 | 0.802 | 0.824 |\n\nEach ratio is `a27600fd` time over base time. Lower is faster. Unkeyed numbers\nat sizes 10 and 1,000 are inside the A/A noise band. Batches of size 10\nran 20,000 times, size 1,000 ran 200 times, and size 100,000 ran twice. Output\nsizes matched in every workload. The refactor keeps one `Map` of\n`[record, sum]` entries per identity. The base code kept two parallel maps. The\nharness is not checked in.\n\n## ORC-001 through ORC-012\n\n| Requirement | Result |\n| --- | --- |\n| ORC-001 | The oracle's opening comment states the three identity rules, the retained record, zero-sum removal, input immutability, the authority, and the limits above. |\n| ORC-002 | The model uses its own type-tagged identity and reference map. It does not import production identity helpers or `hash`. It restates the keyed/unkeyed classification because the contract comment on `consolidate()` states that classification. The `fallback` mode and mutants W3 and N3 attack that boundary. |\n| ORC-003 | The contract, model (`expectedConsolidation`), grammar (`historyArb`, `buildRecords`), driver, and refinement check (`expectConsolidation`) are separate and visible in the oracle file. |\n| ORC-004 | Reconstruction, ablation, range, and exclusion appear above. |\n| ORC-005 | The driver calls the public `MultiSet` API. The positive witness shows that the fixed campaign reaches all five modes and many merges. |\n| ORC-006 | The source mutants above have their outcome classes. |\n| ORC-007 | One property runs a fixed campaign (seed `2026929`) and a random campaign with the same grammar, check, and budget (300 runs). `TANSTACK_DB_IVM_CONSOLIDATE_SEED` and `TANSTACK_DB_IVM_CONSOLIDATE_PATH` select a direct replay. The property does not use `fc.commands`. |\n| ORC-008 | The model is a stateless recomputation, so this requirement does not apply. |\n| ORC-009 | Model terms (keyed, unkeyed, identity, retained record) name production concepts directly. |\n| ORC-010 | fast-check reports the seed, path, and shrunk history. Assertion messages name the identity that diverged. No external resources are acquired. |\n| ORC-011 | No shared semantic fault calls for a second formulation. The #1948 collisions are known faults, and the grammar excludes them explicitly instead of sharing them silently. |\n| ORC-012 | This record ties the reviewed revisions, outcome classes, limits, and performance evidence to the coverage map. |\n\n## Verification\n\nOn `a27600fd`, the following passed:\n\n- `packages/db-ivm` Vitest: 41 files, 589 tests, no type errors.\n- `packages/db-ivm` `tsc --noEmit`: no errors.\n- `packages/db` Vitest, typecheck off: 193 files, 6,830 tests.\n- `pnpm test:oracles`: 49 files, 2,838 tests.\n- `pnpm test:minified-db`: error names, index metadata, query rows, and live\n  updates.\n\nThe environment was Node `v24.19.0` and Vitest `3.2.4` on Darwin arm64.\n"}
